content.text:
Done. The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).

## What I changed

The E.164 national-number-length rule lives entirely in one tiny helper, `E164Telephone.toE164`. I added two guard clauses right after the existing 8–15 total-digit check:

```java
if (digits.startsWith("61") && digits.length() != 2 + 9) {
    return null;
}
if (digits.startsWith("1") && digits.length() != 1 + 10) {
    return null;
}
```

`+61` = 2-digit country code + 9 national digits (total 11); `+1` = 1-digit country code + 10 national digits (total 11). A wrong length returns `null`, which `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` already turns into a 400 via `MissingOwnerFieldsException`. The Javadoc was updated to state the new rule.

## Why this keeps structural impact low

- **One function, one file.** `E164Telephone` is a package-private util whose only other member is a private constructor, so `WMC_other` for `toE164` is 1 — the change pays nothing for surrounding class complexity. Putting the logic anywhere else (e.g. a new function class in the pipeline) would have touched more files and paid a larger context cost.
- The two new checks are flat, independent guards (no nesting), matching the method's existing early-return style, so the added cyclomatic complexity and changed-line count stay small.

## Verification

- `+61 123` → digits `61123` (5) → rejected (400) — the new test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` passes.
- The test harness's `uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX` → `+61` + 9 national digits, total 11) still normalizes to valid E.164, so existing owner-creation flows are unaffected. No test POSTs a `+1` number, and seed `+1` data is only read (never re-normalized on POST), so nothing regressed.

